# Saving Graphics

## Saving Graphics {.smaller}

::: {.columns .pv4}

::: {.column width="60%"}
-   `ggsave()` exports ggplots to files
    -   We control the exact size and format

::: {.fragment .mt1}
-   [Raster]{.b .blue} ([png]{.b}, jpg, bmp, tif): Compatibility


::: {.mt1}
-   [Vector]{.b .blue} ([pdf]{.b}, svg, wmf, eps): Scalability
:::

![](../../img/formats.png)

:::
:::

::: {.column .tc .pv4 width="40%"}
{{< lif "../../icons/polaroid.json" trigger=hover colors=secondary:#2a76dd class=rc >}}
:::
:::

## Using the ggsave function


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Prepare a graphic and assign it to an object (p)
p <- ggplot(mpg, aes(x = displ, y = hwy)) + geom_point()
```
:::


:::{.columns}
:::{.column}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Save to a raster (png) file
ggsave(
  filename = "raster.png",
  plot = p,
  width = 9,
  height = 7,
  units = "in"
)
```
:::

:::
:::{.column}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Save to a vector (pdf) file
ggsave(
  filename = "vector.pdf",
  plot = p,
  width = 9,
  height = 7,
  units = "in"
)
```
:::

:::
:::

